Ever wondered what a day in the life of a DevOps engineer really looks like? Spoiler: It’s not all cloud and glory.

The life of a DevOps engineer can be summed up in one word — why.

Here’s a glimpse of how most days unfold:

𝐀𝐭 𝟗 𝐀𝐌, I log in to find Jenkins already failing. I haven’t even had my morning chai, but the pipeline gave up before I did.

𝐁𝐲 𝟏𝟎:𝟑𝟎, I’m trying to deploy a microservice. YAML throws an “unexpected indent” error. I throw an unexpected breakdown.

𝐀𝐭 𝐧𝐨𝐨𝐧, it’s time for the daily stand-up.
Manager: “Is everything on track?”
Me: “Yes, totally.”
My terminal: 404 – Hope not found.

𝐋𝐮𝐧𝐜𝐡 𝐚𝐭 𝟏:𝟑𝟎? Not happening.
Slack says production is down.
The CPU is at 97 percent, and the team’s morale is nowhere to be seen.

𝐁𝐲 𝟑 𝐏𝐌, I fix one bug only to discover two new ones.
I call this DevOps multiplication.

𝐀𝐭 𝟔 𝐏𝐌, it's time to deploy.
I run terraform apply with all the hope I’ve got left.
It fails. “Resource already exists.” Naturally.

𝐋𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐫 𝐚𝐭 𝐧𝐢𝐠𝐡𝐭, I see a post saying someone became a Cloud Architect at 24.
I’m still trying to figure out why my Kubernetes pod won’t restart.

Truth is, DevOps is more than a job — it’s a daily survival challenge with Jenkins, YAML, and caffeine as your only tools.

And somehow, despite it all, we keep coming back to it.

If you’re in DevOps, you know the feeling.
If you’re just entering it — welcome to the ride.
